  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on regional data from Germany and may not directly generalize to other geographic areas with different species ecology or traffic patterns.",
      "what": "This study analyzed wildlife-vehicle collision (WVC) data from Germany alongside hourly traffic estimates to evaluate how daylight-saving time (DST) shifts traffic intensity and alters collision risks. The analysis revealed that shifting traffic into dawn during spring led to higher-than-expected collision rates, with the most pronounced variations in WVC responses occurring during dawn hours.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that the impact of daylight-saving time on wildlife collisions is not uniform and depends heavily on regional conditions and seasonal animal behavior, showing that traffic intensity alone cannot predict collision risks."
    }
